---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";
import { Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Mechanics - Replenish Guide"
    description="Learn the complex rules interactions of the Replenish deck. Master the timing of Parallax Wave, Opalescence triggers, and how to permanently exile your opponent’s creatures."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="Deck Mechanics"
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/opalescence.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Opalescence"
        imageCaption="Opalescence by John Avon"
        isCard={true}
    />
    <Card
        title="Core Combos & Locks"
        titleTag="h2"
        style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"
    >
        <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Opal-Wave Permanent Exile</h3>
        <p>
            With <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence"
                >Opalescence</a
            > and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave"
                >Parallax Wave</a
            > on the battlefield, Wave becomes an operational 4/4 creature capable
            of targeting itself. You retain priority and stack consecutive creature-exile
            triggers against opposing threats, allocating the final fading counter
            to target Wave itself. Wave exits and immediately returns with five fresh
            fading counters as a brand-new object. Subsequently, the original exile
            triggers resolve on the stack, eradicating those targets permanently.
            Repeat this sequence until the opposing battlefield is cleared.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Combat Sequencing:</strong> A mechanical consequence of this loop
            is that <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
            > re-enters as a new permanent subject to summoning sickness, preventing
            it from attacking that turn. Therefore, whenever tactically possible,
            temporarily exile opposing blockers before combat, execute a full team
            attack (swinging with Wave for 4 damage), and initiate the permanent exile
            loop during your post-combat main phase. The notable exception occurs
            when opposing creatures carry devastating Enter the Battlefield (ETB)
            triggers (such as <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Goblin Ringleader">Goblin Ringleader</a
            > or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Bone Shredder"
                >Bone Shredder</a
            >). Because temporary exile permits them to re-enter and re-trigger
            before permanent removal, immediately permanently exile such threats
            prior to combat.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Exiling Opposing Enchantments:</strong> Because <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
            > globally animates <em>all</em> non-Aura enchantments on the battlefield,
            opposing enchantments transform into targetable creatures. Consequently,
            you can utilize the Wave interaction to permanently exile antagonistic
            permanents such as <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Survival of the Fittest"
                >Survival of the Fittest</a
            >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Sylvan Library"
                >Sylvan Library</a
            >, or an opposing <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
            >.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Opalescence & Summoning Sickness</h3>
        <p>
            A frequent analytical misstep among inexperienced pilots is assuming
            a resolved <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish"
                >Replenish</a
            > enables immediate lethal combat. Because <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
            > animates returned enchantments into creatures, any permanent entering
            the battlefield this turn is governed by summoning sickness and cannot
            attack. Consequently, executing a decisive board lock via <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
            > and <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
            > is essential to completely insulate your position across a full turn
            cycle before delivering lethal damage.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">The Decree Lock</h3>
        <p>
            Post-<a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish"
                >Replenish</a
            > with all three structural anchors assembled (<a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
            >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave"
                >Parallax Wave</a
            >, and <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
            >), all opposing creatures are systematically exiled and every
            inbound spell is unconditionally countered.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Resetting Decree with Wave</h3>
        <p>
            When <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
            > counters its third spell, its mandatory sacrifice trigger stacks. In
            response, activate an animated <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
            > to target the Decree. Upon resolving the standard flicker loop, Decree
            returns with three refreshed depletion counters. While this sequence briefly
            exposes a tiny window on the stack, an opponent would need to cast three
            distinct spells simply to force the third trigger and still retain instant-speed
            interaction to exploit the transition—an exceptional hurdle for traditional
            Premodern archetypes. Even if removal targeting Opalescence resolves during
            this brief gap, Decree simply returns as an unenchantment object ready
            to counter their next three plays.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Value Replenish</h3>
        <p>
            Championship matches do not strictly require assembling the complete
            three-piece lock. Resolving <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
            > purely for incremental tactical value—such as reanimating a solitary
            <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Decree of Silence"
                >Decree of Silence</a
            > to asphyxiate an opponent holding high-mana spells, or recurring <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
            > paired with <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
            > to completely dominate creature boards—is frequently sufficient to clinch
            decisive victories.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Protecting Your Board</h3>
        <p>
            If an opponent resolves mass sweeping removal such as <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Tranquil Domain">Tranquil Domain</a
            > or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Wrath of God"
                >Wrath of God</a
            >, retain priority while <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
            > is active to exile your own critical enchantment creatures via <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
            >. As the sweeper resolves and destroys Wave, all exiled permanents
            safely re-enter completely refreshed. Similarly, when controlling
            dual Opalescence instances against targeted interaction, simply
            activating Wave to flicker the targeted copy invalidates the inbound
            removal spell.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Parallax Wave + Disenchant Effect</h3>
        <p>
            Independent of Opalescence, you can engineer permanent creature
            removal utilizing <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
            > or sideboard <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
            > effects. Activate <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
            > to target up to five opposing creatures. Retaining priority with those
            targeting activations on the stack, sacrifice your Seal (or cast Disenchant)
            destroying your own Wave. As Wave exits the battlefield prior to exiling
            anything, its leaves-play clause resolves immediately (returning zero
            creatures). Finally, the original exile activations resolve, permanently
            expelling those targets—a paramount defensive stabilization maneuver during
            early turns.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card
        title="Synergies & Tricks"
        titleTag="h2"
        style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"
    >
        <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Parallax Wave Creature Synergies</h3>
        <p>
            Beyond clearing adversarial boards, <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
            > exhibits profound synergy with your own creature packages, particularly
            post-sideboard. <em
                >Note: The initial two maneuvers rely on active <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                > and Wave presence to execute immediate loop flickers.</em
            >
        </p>
        <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                <strong>Flipping Exalted Angel for Free:</strong> Deploying <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                > face-down via morph for three colorless mana permits targeting it
                with <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                >. Executing an Opal-Wave flicker immediately returns the Angel
                to the battlefield face-up, entirely circumventing the 2WW
                unmorph cost while presenting an immediate 4/5 flying lifelink
                threat.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                <strong>Resetting Meddling Mage:</strong> If an opponent reveals alternative
                threat avenues or circumvents your primary named restriction, target
                your own <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                > with Wave before initiating an immediate flicker loop. Upon re-entering,
                Mage permits declaring an entirely new target name tailored to the
                evolving board state.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                <strong>Creature Protection (Safe-Housing):</strong> Utilizing Wave
                independently without Opalescence allows you to "safe-house" vulnerable
                creatures like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                > or <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                > in response to opposing lethal sweepers or spot removal. Exiling
                your own creatures shelters them until Wave naturally depletes its
                fading counters later in the game, returning your clock unharmed.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Asymmetrical Tsabo's Web</h3>
        <p>
            Competitive Replenish relies on <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a
            > to paralyze disruptive opposing lands including <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Rishadan Port">Rishadan Port</a
            >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Wasteland"
                >Wasteland</a
            >, and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Dust Bowl"
                >Dust Bowl</a
            >. This restriction remains completely asymmetrical because Tsabo's
            Web strictly targets lands possessing activated abilities that <em
                >do not</em
            > generate mana. All native non-basic filtering sources within our architecture
            (<a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Skycloud Expanse"
                >Skycloud Expanse</a
            >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Adarkar Wastes"
                >Adarkar Wastes</a
            >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Ancient Tomb"
                >Ancient Tomb</a
            >) exclusively carry mana-generating abilities, untapping
            effortlessly under the lock.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Frantic Search Mana Tricks</h3>
        <p>
            Beyond its role as a high-velocity discard engine, the untap clause
            within <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
            > unlocks pivotal logistical combat mana tricks:
        </p>
        <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                <strong>Netting Mana via Ancient Tomb:</strong> While Frantic Search
                conventionally operates as a zero-net-cost discard mechanism, resolving
                it when untapping <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Ancient Tomb">Ancient Tomb</a
                > actively ramps mana production. For example, tapping three lands
                (including Tomb) floats 4 mana; spending 3 on <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > leaves 1 mana floating before untapping those exact three lands.
                You now command 5 available mana on turn three—enabling an immediate
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish"
                    >Replenish</a
                > while preserving open interaction mana.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                <strong>Bypassing Rishadan Port:</strong> When an opponent activates
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Rishadan Port"
                    >Rishadan Port</a
                > during your upkeep, float the targeted land's mana in response.
                Following resolution, utilize that floating mana to cast <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                >, immediately untapping your disrupted lands and neutralizing
                their interactive tempo play.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                <strong>Bypassing Tangle Wire Taxes:</strong> When confronting an
                opposing <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tangle Wire">Tangle Wire</a
                > tap trigger during your upkeep, float all available mana across
                your targeted lands in response. Permit the tap trigger to resolve,
                exhausting your board state, and then immediately expend the floating
                mana on <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > to cleanly re-untap three core lands for main-phase execution.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">Attunement's Built-in Protection</h3>
        <p>
            The activation syntax of <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
            > explicitly states: "Return Attunement to its owner's hand: Draw three
            cards, then discard four cards." Because bouncing the enchantment back
            to your hand represents the mandatory activation <em>cost</em> (rather
            than an effect residing on the stack), you can freely activate it in immediate
            response to inbound removal like <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
            > or an opposing <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
            >. Attunement immediately returns to safety in your
            hand—invalidating their interaction spell completely while
            guaranteeing full card-filtering velocity.
        </p>
    </Card>
    <DecklistTooltip />
</Layout>
